# What a refused round's own words teach the next round, before a handle's
# tag is asked (design.md §4.19; `cli/rounds.hero`): a bound function whose
# header's function-like macro clang refused at the program's own C, called
# by its name in parentheses (`emit/call_spelling.hero`, defect 2511), and a
# callback whose header writes its integers with C's other words, handed C a
# thunk of those words (`emit/callback_learn.hero`, lane p261-beside,
# 2026-11-26). Each lesson only grows the build's table, so a round that
# learns nothing ends the rounds. Out of `cli/rounds.hero` the day the
# second lesson came, which took that file past its 300 lines.

use cli/compile
use cli/pointee_ask
use emit/call_spelling
use emit/callback_learn
use source

# How many lessons the refused round's `refusals` taught, into the table.
function learned(s: source.Source, art: compile.Artifact, answered: [pointee_ask.Answered], refusals: [str], @struct_tags: {str: bool}) -> i64
    called = call_spelling.learn(s, art.decls, refusals: refusals, struct_tags: @struct_tags)
    handed = callback_learn.learn(
        s,
        art.decls,
        p: art.program,
        c: art.checked,
        answered: answered,
        refusals: refusals,
        struct_tags: @struct_tags
    )
    return called + handed
